1
00:00:05,923 --> 00:00:08,217
[narrator, in English] Sedona, Arizona.

2
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:21,897
[Noel, in Japanese] It's
almost time to be with everyone again.

3
00:00:22,606 --> 00:00:25,234
This may be
the first time we've been apart this long.

4
00:00:26,652 --> 00:00:28,153
I wonder how they're doing?

5
00:00:29,112 --> 00:00:30,864
It's the special
summer break Noel organized

6
00:00:30,948 --> 00:00:32,783
for the Travis Japan members.

7
00:00:32,866 --> 00:00:36,578
At the foot of the Rocky Mountains
where the desert winds blow

8
00:00:36,662 --> 00:00:40,415
lies the real America
the members have yet to see.

9
00:00:44,253 --> 00:00:47,381
The Travis Japan members
except for Noel divide up into two groups

10
00:00:47,464 --> 00:00:49,716
and head to their final destination.
Sedona, Arizona.

11
00:00:54,888 --> 00:00:58,308
Noel sets out to Sedona on his own
before the others

12
00:00:58,392 --> 00:01:00,727
to quietly prepare
a warm welcome for them.

13
00:01:16,785 --> 00:01:19,746
Travis Japan
SUMMER VACATION!! IN THE USA

14
00:01:26,753 --> 00:01:32,759
[in Japanese] Team A is closing
in on Sedona, where Noel awaits.

15
00:01:34,845 --> 00:01:38,557
With time to kill before the reunion,

16
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:42,603
they decide
to make a quick detour to a nearby town.

17
00:01:45,105 --> 00:01:47,190
[in English] Flagstaff, Arizona.

18
00:01:47,983 --> 00:01:50,235
[in Japanese] It's been a while
since we last saw a train.

19
00:01:50,319 --> 00:01:54,031
Come to think of it, we've hardly seen
any railroad tracks this trip.

20
00:01:54,114 --> 00:01:55,532
This is some kind of a station, right?

21
00:01:55,615 --> 00:01:57,576
[Umi] This is the Flagstaff station…

22
00:01:57,659 --> 00:01:59,786
[Chaka] I wonder
if travelers come here by trains.

23
00:01:59,870 --> 00:02:01,121
-Good point.
-This is amazing.

24
00:02:01,204 --> 00:02:02,873
You know what this reminds me of?

25
00:02:02,956 --> 00:02:03,957
Stand By Me.

26
00:02:04,041 --> 00:02:06,251
That's right,
they walk along the train tracks.

27
00:02:06,335 --> 00:02:09,421
-[Shizu] I saw a train pass through.
-It's such a picturesque town.

28
00:02:09,504 --> 00:02:11,965
-It should be featured in a movie.
-[Umi] This place has great ambience.

29
00:02:12,049 --> 00:02:13,800
[Chaka] Do you think sleeper trains
pass through here?

30
00:02:13,884 --> 00:02:15,177
-[Umi] I'd say.
-[Shizu] Not a bad guess.

31
00:02:15,260 --> 00:02:19,139
Like… maybe you could get
around by trains?

32
00:02:19,222 --> 00:02:22,100
-What an adventure that'd be.
-There might be a train culture like that.

33
00:02:22,184 --> 00:02:24,353
-Well, shall we get going?
-[both] Let's go.

34
00:02:31,693 --> 00:02:32,903
[Chaka] Umi…

35
00:02:33,528 --> 00:02:34,529
you've leveled up.

36
00:02:34,613 --> 00:02:36,615
By that,
you mean his fashion. Look at your neck.

37
00:02:36,698 --> 00:02:38,575
I stumbled
upon so many wonderful accessories.

38
00:02:38,659 --> 00:02:40,702
-Great style. I love it.
-See?

39
00:02:40,786 --> 00:02:43,497
-This one's silver, and this here's gold.
-You're blinged-out.

40
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:46,458
I'm wearing five,
and that's just necklaces.

41
00:02:46,541 --> 00:02:48,335
-No friggin' way.
-You've got so much bling.

42
00:02:48,418 --> 00:02:50,128
-I'm decked out.
-But I like this one.

43
00:02:50,212 --> 00:02:51,713
Check it out. It's reversible.

44
00:02:51,797 --> 00:02:52,923
Whoa.

45
00:02:53,632 --> 00:02:57,135
[Umi] I could go for some coffee. You?

46
00:02:57,219 --> 00:02:59,304
-Let's sip coffee as we stroll.
-[Shizu] Let's grab coffee, then.

47
00:03:00,097 --> 00:03:01,556
What a fancy place.

48
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:03,141
It's much larger than I expected.

49
00:03:04,309 --> 00:03:05,310
Here's an idea.

50
00:03:06,478 --> 00:03:08,939
Before we head to our next stop,

51
00:03:09,022 --> 00:03:12,943
you want
to grab coffee and explore the town, yeah?

52
00:03:13,026 --> 00:03:14,152
How about this?

53
00:03:14,236 --> 00:03:15,529
We play rock, paper, scissors

54
00:03:15,612 --> 00:03:19,074
-and the loser's on coffee duty.
-I'm in.

55
00:03:19,157 --> 00:03:21,076
-The winners check the city out.
-We'll check it out.

56
00:03:21,159 --> 00:03:22,327
-Ready?
-The loser covers the tab?

57
00:03:22,411 --> 00:03:23,829
-Loser fetches coffee.
-The winner pays.

58
00:03:23,912 --> 00:03:25,455
-Deal.
-We've gotta let the winner pay.

59
00:03:25,539 --> 00:03:28,083
-Winner pays tabs Flagstaff roshambo?
-[exclaims]

60
00:03:28,166 --> 00:03:30,502
Ready? Winner pays tabs Flagstaff,
rock, paper, scissors…

61
00:03:30,585 --> 00:03:33,755
-Wait, wait…
-What the heck, that wasn't fair!

62
00:03:33,839 --> 00:03:35,924
-I thought… I won…
-The timing was wrong.

63
00:03:36,007 --> 00:03:37,008
That was cheating.

64
00:03:37,092 --> 00:03:38,927
-Making excuses now, huh?
-What a sore winner.

65
00:03:39,010 --> 00:03:41,513
-You won't fetch us coffee, then?
-Hold on, I'm not complaining.

66
00:03:41,596 --> 00:03:44,641
-But are you sure about that round?
-Me? No, it didn't seem fair to me.

67
00:03:44,724 --> 00:03:46,059
-Okay, then.
-Last chance, okay?

68
00:03:46,143 --> 00:03:47,394
Here.

69
00:03:47,477 --> 00:03:49,104
-Ready?
-Ready, go.

70
00:03:49,187 --> 00:03:51,314
[all] The winner covers
the tab, rock, paper, scissors.

71
00:03:51,398 --> 00:03:54,067
-Man, I wanted to play lackey.
-How the table turns.

72
00:03:54,151 --> 00:03:56,736
-[Umi] Oh, shoot. You're a real man.
-Yep.

73
00:03:56,820 --> 00:03:59,698
-This is what I get for suggesting it!
-[laughing]

74
00:03:59,781 --> 00:04:01,241
You shot yourself in the foot with that.

75
00:04:01,324 --> 00:04:02,576
-This is hard to watch.
-I'll man up.

76
00:04:02,659 --> 00:04:04,077
-Just coffee, yeah?
-That's right.

77
00:04:04,161 --> 00:04:06,413
-And only small servings.
-Yep, a short will do.

78
00:04:06,496 --> 00:04:08,206
Wouldn't you rather have
a large if they have one?

79
00:04:08,290 --> 00:04:10,542
-I prefer a bigger size, but…
-It'll be too bulky to carry around.

80
00:04:10,625 --> 00:04:12,919
-You're right. It'll get in the way.
-Well, I'll leave it to you.

81
00:04:13,753 --> 00:04:15,755
-I'll find somewhere to grab coffee.
-Where are you going?

82
00:04:15,839 --> 00:04:17,382
-You can walk around.
-Okay.

83
00:04:19,718 --> 00:04:22,429
Well, there we go.
That's everyone's coffee.

84
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:25,432
I'm the one who suggested it, but…

85
00:04:28,018 --> 00:04:29,019
[in English] Excuse me.

86
00:04:33,356 --> 00:04:35,358
[in Japanese] Man, this is pathetic.

87
00:04:36,818 --> 00:04:39,946
Here I am,
everyone's drinks with both hands.

88
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:49,080
[in English] It is so beautiful.

89
00:04:50,207 --> 00:04:52,501
-[in Japanese] How pretty. See?
-It's a work of art.

90
00:04:53,293 --> 00:04:55,212
-Ain't it something?
-Right?

91
00:04:55,796 --> 00:04:57,839
There's no way someone made this by hand.

92
00:04:57,923 --> 00:04:59,758
-[Umi] Such craftsmanship.
-This is legit artwork.

93
00:05:00,342 --> 00:05:02,344
[in English]
And then he does the Monument…

94
00:05:02,427 --> 00:05:04,971
-Monument Valley.
-Monument Valley? Wow.

95
00:05:05,055 --> 00:05:06,848
-Yeah, Monument Valley.
-We went to.

96
00:05:06,932 --> 00:05:08,517
-Yes.
-[Umi, in Japanese] That's incredible.

97
00:05:12,687 --> 00:05:13,897
Whoa.

98
00:05:31,039 --> 00:05:32,666
-Hey, isn't it cute?
-Which one?

99
00:05:34,167 --> 00:05:35,168
Wow.

100
00:05:36,211 --> 00:05:39,172
Shoot. I've got a feeling
it's another fateful encounter.

101
00:05:39,256 --> 00:05:41,591
Add that to the collection. [laughs]

102
00:05:41,675 --> 00:05:45,303
-[Shizu, in English] Wow.
-[Umi] May I try this bangle?

103
00:05:45,387 --> 00:05:46,388
[worker] Yeah.

104
00:05:48,765 --> 00:05:50,267
Holy cow. Could you hold this
for me, Shizuya?

105
00:05:50,350 --> 00:05:51,351
[Shizu] Of course.

106
00:05:53,603 --> 00:05:54,729
[Umi, in English] Thank you.

107
00:05:54,813 --> 00:05:55,814
[worker] So you go… Yeah.

108
00:05:55,897 --> 00:05:58,608
-Exactly. Kind of on the outside.
-[Umi, in Japanese] This one's legit.

109
00:05:59,484 --> 00:06:00,485
It's…

110
00:06:01,319 --> 00:06:02,445
There we go.

111
00:06:02,529 --> 00:06:03,905
[Shizu] I love the design.

112
00:06:03,989 --> 00:06:06,408
-[worker, in English] That's beautiful.
-[Umi] Yeah, it's beautiful.

113
00:06:07,826 --> 00:06:09,786
-[in Japanese] And the collection grows.
-Isn't it sick?

114
00:06:11,746 --> 00:06:14,249
I can't believe I stumbled
upon such a beautiful gem.

115
00:06:15,292 --> 00:06:16,293
I'm delighted.

116
00:06:16,376 --> 00:06:17,794
-[Shizu] You look happy.
-Yep.

117
00:06:17,878 --> 00:06:19,963
-My heart skipped a beat when I saw it.
-[chuckles]

118
00:06:20,046 --> 00:06:21,715
Chaka… He's here alone. Huh?

119
00:06:22,257 --> 00:06:24,342
-Thanks… Thank you very much!
-Thank you, thank you!

120
00:06:24,426 --> 00:06:25,760
-Thank you very much.
-I feel terrible.

121
00:06:25,844 --> 00:06:26,845
-Boss!
-Hey, Boss.

122
00:06:26,928 --> 00:06:28,805
-Enjoy your drink.
-Seriously, sorry about the trouble.

123
00:06:29,598 --> 00:06:30,599
Then I'll take one.

124
00:06:31,641 --> 00:06:33,268
-Thanks.
-Thanks for going out of your way.

125
00:06:33,351 --> 00:06:34,352
Time for a sip.

126
00:06:35,812 --> 00:06:37,188
Do you want to explore the area?

127
00:06:37,272 --> 00:06:39,482
-Huh? What? Hey… Sorry.
-Huh? What? What's the matter?

128
00:06:39,566 --> 00:06:41,151
-Are you upset?
-Excuse me…

129
00:06:41,234 --> 00:06:42,444
-Chaka…
-Let's go, Sensei.

130
00:06:42,527 --> 00:06:45,363
…do you want to put your trash
in here? I'll take the cup holder.

131
00:06:45,447 --> 00:06:46,948
-Thanks.
-Are you upset, Sensei?

132
00:06:51,244 --> 00:06:52,579
-Wait, are you pouting?
-He's upset.

133
00:06:52,662 --> 00:06:53,663
Not at all.

134
00:06:55,206 --> 00:06:56,583
What gives me the right?

135
00:06:56,666 --> 00:06:58,627
-Then at least act like you're not mad.
-It was my idea.

136
00:06:58,710 --> 00:07:00,253
-Be pleasant.
-Be pleasant.

137
00:07:04,924 --> 00:07:07,052
-[Chaka, in Japanese] What a stylish city.
-[Umi] You're right.

138
00:07:07,927 --> 00:07:09,512
[Chaka] This bus stop is cute too.

139
00:07:09,596 --> 00:07:12,098
[Umi] It sure is.
Do you wanna sit for a minute?

140
00:07:12,182 --> 00:07:13,183
[Shizu] I'm up for it.

141
00:07:20,065 --> 00:07:21,858
-We could stage a magazine shoot here.
-How nice.

142
00:07:21,941 --> 00:07:22,942
Right?

143
00:07:25,987 --> 00:07:27,530
It's got a proper roof.

144
00:07:27,614 --> 00:07:28,615
It's the best.

145
00:07:29,449 --> 00:07:30,700
What a charming little bus stop.

146
00:07:35,747 --> 00:07:38,083
[Umi] Where are we headed again?
Something "owl cafe," right?

147
00:07:38,166 --> 00:07:39,959
-"Toasted," I think.
-I've been looking for it.

148
00:07:40,043 --> 00:07:41,920
What does
the English word, "toasted", mean?

149
00:07:42,003 --> 00:07:43,463
It could mean
"toast," as in toasted bread.

150
00:07:43,546 --> 00:07:45,256
-It doesn't have special meaning?
-I guess "toast"

151
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:48,301
-must refer to toasting something.
-This place is packed.

152
00:07:48,385 --> 00:07:49,386
-There it is.
-Right?

153
00:07:49,469 --> 00:07:52,514
-[Shizu] They went crazy with the owls.
-"Toasted Owl."

154
00:07:52,597 --> 00:07:54,349
[Umi] See? There are owls everywhere.

155
00:07:54,432 --> 00:07:56,559
[Chaka] "Owl" must refer
to the bird, "owl."

156
00:07:58,103 --> 00:08:01,022
-[Shizu] Let's check it out.
-Is it awake or asleep?

157
00:08:01,648 --> 00:08:03,817
It's definitely awake.
It's more alert than the other ones.

158
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:04,901
Does it go, "hoot"?

159
00:08:04,984 --> 00:08:08,196
You're right. Their eyes are bulging out.
Bloodshot, even.

160
00:08:08,279 --> 00:08:10,281
-[Shizu] Let's enter.
-[Chaka] Well, here we are.

161
00:08:11,074 --> 00:08:14,244
-[all, in English] Hi.
-[Chaka] Three of us.

162
00:08:14,327 --> 00:08:15,870
-[server] Three?
-Yes.

163
00:08:16,788 --> 00:08:18,081
-[Shizu] Hey.
-How are you?

164
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:20,083
[Umi] Thank you.

165
00:08:24,546 --> 00:08:25,922
-What a hearty serving!
-Taco salad?

166
00:08:26,005 --> 00:08:27,465
-Yeah.
-Isn't it awesome?

167
00:08:27,549 --> 00:08:28,591
[chuckles]

168
00:08:28,675 --> 00:08:31,010
-Oh, yeah. Thank you.
-And tacos for you as well.

169
00:08:31,094 --> 00:08:32,637
And here's
your side of eggs, if you'd like.

170
00:08:32,721 --> 00:08:34,305
-Oh, that'll be mine.
-Or that's you?

171
00:08:34,389 --> 00:08:35,974
-Yeah.
-No, no, no. Okay. There you are.

172
00:08:36,057 --> 00:08:37,517
-Thank you.
-Uh-huh. Enjoy.

173
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:38,768
-Thank you so much.
-Yeah.

174
00:08:38,852 --> 00:08:40,145
-We have question…
-Okay?

175
00:08:40,228 --> 00:08:45,734
…of why the name say "Toasted Owl."

176
00:08:45,817 --> 00:08:46,860
-Toasted Owl?
-Yeah.

177
00:08:46,943 --> 00:08:50,613
Um, I used to be a teacher,
and… during the Harry Potter days.

178
00:08:50,697 --> 00:08:53,074
-Uh-huh.
-And so all the kids brought me owls

179
00:08:53,158 --> 00:08:56,202
because I said I loved
the owl in Harry Potter.

180
00:08:56,286 --> 00:08:57,579
And it got kind of weird.

181
00:08:57,662 --> 00:08:58,830
I had too many owls.

182
00:08:58,913 --> 00:09:02,208
And so I stopped teaching
and I decided to open a restaurant.

183
00:09:02,292 --> 00:09:04,294
-[Umi] Whoa!
-Whoa.

184
00:09:04,377 --> 00:09:07,213
And with all these owls
and then it just became huge.

185
00:09:07,297 --> 00:09:09,466
So we have three,

186
00:09:09,549 --> 00:09:11,760
and we're opening
a fourth one in about two months.

187
00:09:11,843 --> 00:09:13,887
-So, it's been a lot of fun.
-[exclaiming]

188
00:09:13,970 --> 00:09:17,182
That's inspired by Hedwig?

189
00:09:17,265 --> 00:09:18,892
-Exactly. Uh-huh.
-Uh-huh.

190
00:09:18,975 --> 00:09:21,019
-You know. Harry Potter is the bomb.
-[Umi] Yes.

191
00:09:22,062 --> 00:09:24,230
Well, before you guys leave,
pick out a T-shirt.

192
00:09:24,314 --> 00:09:27,442
It's on the house,
and you can wear the owl out…

193
00:09:27,525 --> 00:09:30,403
-Home, and it's… I'm glad you're here.
-Ah.

194
00:09:30,487 --> 00:09:31,738
Thank you very, very much.

195
00:09:31,821 --> 00:09:33,990
[Chaka] That's souvenir, great souvenir.

196
00:09:34,074 --> 00:09:36,034
It is a good souvenir.
You'll always think of the owl.

197
00:09:36,117 --> 00:09:37,702
-[Umi] Yeah, you look cute.
-Ah, so cute.

198
00:09:37,786 --> 00:09:39,037
You so talented.

199
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:41,081
-Oh, I don't… No, I'm just a…
-Yeah. [chuckles]

200
00:09:41,581 --> 00:09:44,709
-I'm just really funny, can I like…
-[chuckling]

201
00:09:44,793 --> 00:09:46,795
-Thank you so much.
-Thank you so much. Enjoy your meal.

202
00:09:46,878 --> 00:09:48,254
-Yeah, appreciate it.
-Enjoy.

203
00:09:48,338 --> 00:09:50,340
-[Umi, in Japanese] It looks decadent.
-[Shizu] Amazing.

204
00:09:50,423 --> 00:09:52,008
This cafe owner drew
inspiration from a movie.

205
00:09:52,092 --> 00:09:54,761
-In other words, a work of art.
-That's what made her want to open shop.

206
00:09:54,844 --> 00:09:56,930
[Chaka] She opened
this cafe through sheer willpower,

207
00:09:57,013 --> 00:09:58,765
and it's now
one of the most popular spots in town.

208
00:09:58,848 --> 00:10:01,935
This is the kind
of town where dreams can come true.

209
00:10:02,018 --> 00:10:03,728
This place is her canvas, so to speak.

210
00:10:03,812 --> 00:10:06,356
[Shizu] Yes.
This town's got a real motley crew.

211
00:10:08,733 --> 00:10:10,985
-Your tacos looks scrumptious.
-[Umi] Well, I mean,

212
00:10:11,069 --> 00:10:14,531
-I've gotta eat tacos, you know?
-[Shizu] Mm-hmm.

213
00:10:14,614 --> 00:10:16,741
-I crave it from time to time too.
-I feel you.

214
00:10:19,786 --> 00:10:20,870
[Shizu] It's delicious.

215
00:10:22,372 --> 00:10:23,498
Yum.

216
00:10:23,581 --> 00:10:24,874
[Shizu] What a cute T-shirt.

217
00:10:24,958 --> 00:10:26,584
-[Umi] Right?
-They even gifted us T-shirts.

218
00:10:26,668 --> 00:10:29,462
-We had many encounters on this trip.
-Yeah, no. It was the best trip ever.

219
00:10:30,213 --> 00:10:31,840
-Now, finally…
-Yes.

220
00:10:32,632 --> 00:10:35,343
That's a wrap for our summer trip.

221
00:10:35,426 --> 00:10:36,511
No joke.

222
00:10:36,594 --> 00:10:39,013
-We were on the road for an entire week.
-Good work, guys.

223
00:10:39,097 --> 00:10:42,183
We're about to regroup
with Team B and Noel, I'm guessing.

224
00:10:42,267 --> 00:10:47,689
Then we'll travel together.
Team A's journey ends here.

225
00:10:47,772 --> 00:10:49,774
Thanks for the ride, guys.
I appreciate you guys.

226
00:10:52,193 --> 00:10:53,611
It was a good one.

227
00:10:59,909 --> 00:11:01,077
-There.
-[grunts]

228
00:11:04,581 --> 00:11:06,624
-Thanks for the adventure.
-Thank you for the good times.

229
00:11:06,708 --> 00:11:07,750
Well then,

230
00:11:08,626 --> 00:11:11,963
we'll join
the rest of the TJ members. Agreed?

231
00:11:12,046 --> 00:11:14,007
[all] Agreed!

232
00:11:14,090 --> 00:11:15,466
[clapping]

233
00:11:15,550 --> 00:11:17,260
-[Shizu] Let's go!
-[Umi] They're clapping for us.

234
00:11:17,343 --> 00:11:18,595
[in English] Thank you.

235
00:11:19,429 --> 00:11:21,222
-Thank you.
-[Shime] Thank you.

236
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:24,225
[in Japanese] Phew, it's hot.

237
00:11:24,309 --> 00:11:25,310
So hot.

238
00:11:26,561 --> 00:11:28,479
-[Machu] Phew.
-Hot, hot.

239
00:11:28,563 --> 00:11:30,190
So, what's on the agenda today?

240
00:11:30,273 --> 00:11:31,316
Today, we are…

241
00:11:32,150 --> 00:11:37,697
"You will take part
in a Native American celebration."

242
00:11:37,780 --> 00:11:38,823
-What's that?
-A celebration?

243
00:11:38,907 --> 00:11:41,284
-A celebration? Is that like, a festival?
-It's a festival!

244
00:11:41,367 --> 00:11:46,623
Yes. "Your timing couldn't have been
any better. You're in luck."

245
00:11:47,248 --> 00:11:49,000
-[laughing]
-Wow, that's a little…

246
00:11:49,083 --> 00:11:51,669
-Well, shall we join the festivities?
-Shall we?

247
00:11:52,212 --> 00:11:53,838
-I'm intrigued. Let's go check it out.
-Yep.

248
00:11:53,922 --> 00:11:55,798
-Let's go. Talk about the sweltering heat.
-No joke.

249
00:11:55,882 --> 00:11:56,966
Shall we get going?

250
00:12:00,553 --> 00:12:02,472
-Machu, are you enjoying your ice cream?
-Hmm?

251
00:12:02,555 --> 00:12:04,432
[muffled] Ice cream tastes
best on a hot summer day.

252
00:12:04,515 --> 00:12:06,142
[chuckling]

253
00:12:06,893 --> 00:12:09,270
-[Genta] Then, let's go!
-[Machu] Let's get going.

254
00:12:21,366 --> 00:12:24,035
-[Genta] Tired of driving yet, Machu?
-It's going great. It's smooth sailing.

255
00:12:24,118 --> 00:12:26,079
-Seriously? Thanks for taking the wheel.
-Yep.

256
00:12:26,162 --> 00:12:27,914
-Thanks from me too.
-Don't mention it.

257
00:12:29,540 --> 00:12:30,541
Are we almost there?

258
00:12:30,625 --> 00:12:33,211
[laughs] Now, don't rush the driver.

259
00:12:33,753 --> 00:12:38,091
[Genta] I wonder
what a Native American festival is like.

260
00:12:38,174 --> 00:12:40,134
-[Shime] Yeah.
-[Machu] I can't imagine it at all.

261
00:12:40,218 --> 00:12:41,219
[Genta] Neither can I.

262
00:12:43,137 --> 00:12:44,138
It's way too hot.

263
00:12:44,222 --> 00:12:45,348
[Machu] Hey, Shizuya.

264
00:12:45,431 --> 00:12:48,268
-I wonder how they're doing.
-[Genta] Those guys, man.

265
00:12:48,351 --> 00:12:50,853
-I miss everyone. Yeah.
-Me too.

266
00:12:51,688 --> 00:12:53,064
I'm really starting to miss them.

267
00:12:53,690 --> 00:12:56,776
-We really are like best friends, huh?
-I think so.

268
00:12:56,859 --> 00:12:58,444
Wild that we can't wait to see them again.

269
00:12:58,528 --> 00:12:59,654
Yeah.

270
00:12:59,737 --> 00:13:01,614
-It felt a little empty without them.
-I know, right?

271
00:13:01,698 --> 00:13:04,409
-I feel it too.
-Not that you two aren't any fun, but…

272
00:13:04,492 --> 00:13:07,245
-Wait, are we not good enough for you?
-You don't think we're good enough?

273
00:13:08,204 --> 00:13:09,289
[sighs]

274
00:13:10,665 --> 00:13:13,710
-That's not what I said.
-Whoa, careful, careful.

275
00:13:13,793 --> 00:13:17,463
-Keep your eyes on the road.
-Seriously though. Focus on the road.

276
00:13:17,547 --> 00:13:18,631
-My bad, my bad.
-[laughing]

277
00:13:18,715 --> 00:13:21,968
-That was lame.
-Yeah, you looked lame. Geez.

278
00:13:22,051 --> 00:13:23,511
[Machu] Sorry to put your lives in danger.

279
00:13:23,594 --> 00:13:25,054
[Shime] Wonder what Zuya-nii is doing.

280
00:13:25,138 --> 00:13:27,598
[Genta] I bet he hasn't stopped
complaining the moment they took off.

281
00:13:27,682 --> 00:13:30,059
I always lift the mood,
calling him "Zuya-nii" and all.

282
00:13:32,186 --> 00:13:33,438
-But he doesn't have Genta and me around.
-Yep.

283
00:13:33,521 --> 00:13:34,689
-[Genta] Right?
-It's true.

284
00:13:35,440 --> 00:13:38,484
-I bet he's a Debbie Downer without us.
-Bet he's complaining nonstop.

285
00:13:38,568 --> 00:13:41,571
This whole trip feels casual,
not like work.

286
00:13:41,654 --> 00:13:43,531
-Yeah.
-My guess is Umi and Chaka

287
00:13:43,614 --> 00:13:45,366
aren't as enthusiastic
as they usually are.

288
00:13:46,993 --> 00:13:49,162
I'm really starting
to wonder how they're doing.

289
00:13:49,245 --> 00:13:51,914
I'm scared for them.
I wonder if they're getting along?

290
00:13:51,998 --> 00:13:54,667
What if Shizuya's mumbling
to himself the entire time?

291
00:13:56,044 --> 00:13:58,254
-I wouldn't rule out that possibility.
-[Shime] Right?

292
00:13:59,672 --> 00:14:00,715
[exclaims]

293
00:14:00,798 --> 00:14:03,051
-[Shime] The horizon stretches endlessly.
-[Genta] What a view.

294
00:14:03,134 --> 00:14:04,469
[Shime] The earth is red here.

295
00:14:04,552 --> 00:14:06,179
-Hey, um…
-It's red.

296
00:14:06,262 --> 00:14:10,349
How do I put it? Is the soil different?
What makes it so red?

297
00:14:10,433 --> 00:14:13,770
It reminds me of the Grand Canyon.
Isn't Grand Canyon this shade of red?

298
00:14:13,853 --> 00:14:16,272
[Shime] True,
it's all sorts of shades of red.

299
00:14:16,355 --> 00:14:18,357
-[Machu] Amazing.
-[Genta] This view is very similar to it.

300
00:14:19,567 --> 00:14:21,444
Look right, then left.

301
00:14:22,403 --> 00:14:24,072
Right, then left.

302
00:14:24,822 --> 00:14:26,574
The view is the same on both sides!

303
00:14:28,159 --> 00:14:29,952
-Look to your right.
-Okay.

304
00:14:30,495 --> 00:14:31,954
-Then look left.
-All right…

305
00:14:32,705 --> 00:14:33,998
-Look right again.
-Yep.

306
00:14:34,916 --> 00:14:36,959
Now, do it really fast.

307
00:14:38,211 --> 00:14:40,213
-What happens then?
-Do it as fast as you can.

308
00:14:41,089 --> 00:14:42,090
Okay.

309
00:14:42,924 --> 00:14:45,093
-Did you get a bit dizzy?
-I sure did. I'm gonna get carsick.

310
00:14:45,176 --> 00:14:46,427
Yeah.

311
00:14:47,553 --> 00:14:49,097
-So don't do that.
-Thanks.

312
00:14:49,180 --> 00:14:50,973
-[Machu, Genta laugh]
-What was that about?

313
00:14:52,558 --> 00:14:54,519
[narrator, in English] Gallup, New Mexico.

314
00:14:56,020 --> 00:15:00,066
[in Japanese] Gallup is a city situated
in the center of the Native American land.

315
00:15:00,149 --> 00:15:02,860
Known as the heart of Indian Country,

316
00:15:02,944 --> 00:15:07,949
numerous tribes such as the Navajo,
Zuni, Apache, and Hopi reside here.

317
00:15:08,032 --> 00:15:11,244
Gallup is home
to the largest Native American

318
00:15:11,327 --> 00:15:14,622
crafts and jewelry wholesale district
in the United States.

319
00:15:14,705 --> 00:15:17,625
The trio head
straightaway to an Indian craft market.

320
00:15:17,708 --> 00:15:19,961
How amazing. Whoa.

321
00:15:20,044 --> 00:15:21,045
No way.

322
00:15:21,796 --> 00:15:23,840
[Genta] This place is packed
with all sorts of things.

323
00:15:24,674 --> 00:15:26,300
-It feels like a museum in here.
-I agree.

324
00:15:26,843 --> 00:15:28,886
-This is remarkable… what?
-No way.

325
00:15:29,387 --> 00:15:30,429
How cool.

326
00:15:30,513 --> 00:15:31,514
[Shime] Cool indeed.

327
00:15:33,182 --> 00:15:34,809
-What a collection.
-This is seriously cool.

328
00:15:34,892 --> 00:15:36,269
Whoa, what a stylish belt.

329
00:15:37,562 --> 00:15:39,897
-There are pieces of turquoise embedded.
-I see it. How cool.

330
00:15:40,982 --> 00:15:43,151
-[in English] Do you wanna see it?
-Yeah, we wanna see.

331
00:15:45,903 --> 00:15:48,281
-[in Japanese] Now that's rad.
-Don't you think it's stylish?

332
00:15:48,364 --> 00:15:50,366
-[Genta, in English] Wow!
-[Machu] Wow!

333
00:15:51,367 --> 00:15:52,743
Wow!

334
00:15:53,953 --> 00:15:56,122
-[in Japanese] No way.
-[in English] So cool.

335
00:15:57,081 --> 00:15:58,166
Very cool.

336
00:15:58,249 --> 00:16:00,585
-[in Japanese] Oh, how cute.
-Isn't it awesome?

337
00:16:00,668 --> 00:16:02,378
-I love it.
-What a great design.

338
00:16:02,461 --> 00:16:05,548
-[Machu] It's your style, Genta.
-[Genta] I want to take it home.

339
00:16:05,631 --> 00:16:07,675
-[in English] How much is it?
-[vendor] We can't.

340
00:16:07,758 --> 00:16:08,759
[Machu] Yeah, yeah.

341
00:16:08,843 --> 00:16:12,013
[vendor] So the judges come in tomorrow
and judge them,

342
00:16:12,096 --> 00:16:15,308
and then they get
ribbons, or not, depending.

343
00:16:15,391 --> 00:16:18,728
And then on Wednesday night,
it's first come first serve.

344
00:16:18,811 --> 00:16:21,105
[in Japanese]
I'm taking note. Item number 387.

345
00:16:22,982 --> 00:16:24,567
-You're locking in its number?
-Number 387.

346
00:16:24,650 --> 00:16:26,444
-Are you gonna get it?
-How interesting.

347
00:16:26,527 --> 00:16:29,113
-So, as I was browsing…
-[Shime] Yeah?

348
00:16:30,114 --> 00:16:32,450
…I noticed the artist's age on the tags.

349
00:16:33,242 --> 00:16:34,535
-Where?
-[in English] Age.

350
00:16:35,077 --> 00:16:36,871
[vendor] So, this one is 13.

351
00:16:36,954 --> 00:16:38,873
[Shime] Thirteen? [in Japanese] Thirteen?

352
00:16:38,956 --> 00:16:40,458
[vendor, in English] This one is 13.

353
00:16:40,541 --> 00:16:42,835
-[Machu] Thirteen. I liked this one too.
-[Shime] That's talent.

354
00:16:42,919 --> 00:16:44,420
[Genta] This kid's 14. That's incredible.

355
00:16:45,087 --> 00:16:46,088
See this one?

356
00:16:46,839 --> 00:16:48,507
-Eight.
-[vendor] Seven.

357
00:16:48,591 --> 00:16:50,218
-Seven?
-Five. Eight.

358
00:16:50,301 --> 00:16:52,470
-[in Japanese] Seven years old!
-Eight, right? Eight here too.

359
00:16:52,553 --> 00:16:53,554
-Wowza.
-Eight.

360
00:16:53,638 --> 00:16:55,932
[narrator] On this day,
works by Native American children,

361
00:16:56,015 --> 00:16:58,976
future artists in making, were on display.

362
00:16:59,060 --> 00:17:01,354
-This one's by a nine year old.
-Basket weaver's just nine.

363
00:17:01,437 --> 00:17:03,439
-[Machu] Nine?
-[vendor] Her parents…

364
00:17:03,522 --> 00:17:05,316
-I love this.
-I love that we have a…

365
00:17:05,399 --> 00:17:07,068
[Machu] You've got
carpet like this at home.

366
00:17:07,151 --> 00:17:09,904
-I've got 'em all. Mmm.
-…a young girl weaving. It's great.

367
00:17:09,987 --> 00:17:12,531
-She's just, uh, eleven.
-Oh.

368
00:17:12,615 --> 00:17:14,408
-Eleven… Eleven?
-Eleven.

369
00:17:14,492 --> 00:17:15,910
-Oh, eleven?
-Yeah.

370
00:17:15,993 --> 00:17:18,746
-[Machu, in Japanese] They're so talented.
-[Genta] Look at them, hard at work.

371
00:17:18,829 --> 00:17:20,623
-[Machu] They've made tons.
-[Shime] That's splendid.

372
00:17:21,249 --> 00:17:24,502
[in English] They're at home
sitting with their grandma or their mom

373
00:17:24,585 --> 00:17:25,670
learning how to weave.

374
00:17:25,753 --> 00:17:27,338
Some… like, they're known for.

375
00:17:27,421 --> 00:17:32,260
So, Navajos, we're silversmithers,
weavers, and then some pottery.

376
00:17:32,343 --> 00:17:35,846
Uh, the plains tribes
up north, they did the beadwork,

377
00:17:36,556 --> 00:17:40,518
um, and a lot
of the leather work. Sioux, Ojibwe.

378
00:17:40,601 --> 00:17:43,938
Many kids are… can be Zuni and Navajo,

379
00:17:44,021 --> 00:17:48,859
so they may learn both traditions because
they're learning from both families.

380
00:17:48,943 --> 00:17:49,944
-Both.
-Oh.

381
00:17:50,778 --> 00:17:52,655
And do their own thing.

382
00:17:52,738 --> 00:17:54,991
-Wow. Genius.
-And you see that with the kids.

383
00:17:55,074 --> 00:17:57,034
-[Machu] Uh-huh.
-They're… They're taking it

384
00:17:57,118 --> 00:17:58,619
and doing it their own way.

385
00:18:00,621 --> 00:18:03,457
Wow. Oh, my God.

386
00:18:07,044 --> 00:18:10,423
So, it would be used
for tobacco and different things.

387
00:18:10,506 --> 00:18:12,466
-And then the symbol.
-[in Japanese] Is this coral?

388
00:18:12,550 --> 00:18:14,719
-[in English] Shark.
-Yeah. A shark, shark.

389
00:18:14,802 --> 00:18:18,097
[vendor] This is also my daughter's.
She handmakes the paper flowers.

390
00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:19,181
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

391
00:18:19,265 --> 00:18:21,475
[vendor] That's our daughter's.
That's Mary Helen's as well.

392
00:18:21,559 --> 00:18:23,185
-Whoa. Nice. Nice to meet you.
-Hello.

393
00:18:23,269 --> 00:18:24,270
Oh!

394
00:18:25,563 --> 00:18:27,273
-Nice meet you.
-Nice to meet you.

395
00:18:28,274 --> 00:18:29,692
You… You made it?

396
00:18:29,775 --> 00:18:31,319
-Yeah. All by myself.
-Really?

397
00:18:31,402 --> 00:18:32,612
Really?

398
00:18:33,654 --> 00:18:36,073
-[Machu, in Japanese] Whoa.
-[Genta] Impressive.

399
00:18:36,157 --> 00:18:37,950
-[in English] You're genius.
-[child] Thanks.

400
00:18:38,034 --> 00:18:39,035
[chuckling]

401
00:18:39,118 --> 00:18:42,705
-[Machu] Look at this piece of work.
-[Genta] I love it. What a cool design.

402
00:18:44,123 --> 00:18:45,499
-You made it?
-Uh-huh.

403
00:18:45,583 --> 00:18:46,834
-Wow.
-Really?

404
00:18:46,917 --> 00:18:48,669
-Yeah.
-So good.

405
00:18:48,753 --> 00:18:50,171
-Thank you.
-[Machu] How old are you?

406
00:18:50,254 --> 00:18:51,756
-Nine.
-[members] Nine?

407
00:18:51,839 --> 00:18:53,215
-What's your name?
-Lance.

408
00:18:53,299 --> 00:18:55,676
-Lance? Nice to… Nice to meet you.
-That's nice.

409
00:18:55,760 --> 00:18:57,470
-Nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you.

410
00:18:57,553 --> 00:18:58,888
[in Japanese] You've got talent, kid.

411
00:18:59,472 --> 00:19:00,765
-[in English] Amazing.
-Cool.

412
00:19:00,848 --> 00:19:01,849
Very cool.

413
00:19:01,932 --> 00:19:03,851
-Thank you.
-Yeah. Amazing.

414
00:19:03,934 --> 00:19:06,103
-Thank you.
-Awesome. You're so genius.

415
00:19:07,063 --> 00:19:09,273
-Thank you so much.
-Thank you.

416
00:19:10,524 --> 00:19:13,069
-[Genta] Have a good one. Thank you.
-[Shime] Have a good day.

417
00:19:13,152 --> 00:19:15,112
-[Shime] Man, how admirable.
-[Genta] How cool.

418
00:19:15,196 --> 00:19:18,074
-[Shime] Whoa.
-[Genta panting]

419
00:19:18,157 --> 00:19:19,825
All the designs are just so cute.

420
00:19:20,618 --> 00:19:22,995
-I love them all.
-[Machu] These are all designs you'd love.

421
00:19:23,079 --> 00:19:24,664
My couch is full of cushions like these.

422
00:19:24,747 --> 00:19:27,249
-[Shime] Your place is full of 'em.
-[Genta] Yeah, no joke.

423
00:19:27,333 --> 00:19:29,335
I bet you'd like that one too, Genta.

424
00:19:29,418 --> 00:19:32,171
-Don't you know it.
-Do you see that cushion?

425
00:19:32,254 --> 00:19:35,633
[Genta] You know me.
I'm a sucker for cushions.

426
00:19:35,716 --> 00:19:37,760
The cushions take
up my couch, there's no space to sit.

427
00:19:37,843 --> 00:19:40,429
-[laughing]
-The Native American patterns

428
00:19:40,513 --> 00:19:42,640
really match the couch.

429
00:19:42,723 --> 00:19:44,934
I saw ones
like these when I went to your place.

430
00:19:45,017 --> 00:19:46,894
-That's right.
-I thought you had one too many.

431
00:19:46,977 --> 00:19:49,021
-They all look like these too.
-You're absolutely right.

432
00:19:49,105 --> 00:19:52,692
There's no space to sit on my couch.
So I end up sitting on the floor.

433
00:19:52,775 --> 00:19:53,776
[laughing]

434
00:19:53,859 --> 00:19:56,445
-You're hilarious.
-That's not how a couch is used.

435
00:19:56,529 --> 00:19:59,115
-It may not be wrong, but…
-But I love that.

436
00:19:59,198 --> 00:20:01,659
-These would definitely match your couch.
-Right?

437
00:20:01,742 --> 00:20:03,202
[in English] Very, very big.

438
00:20:03,285 --> 00:20:06,455
I'm not gonna be able to open this
all the way out 'cause it's too heavy.

439
00:20:06,539 --> 00:20:07,790
Too heavy.

440
00:20:08,916 --> 00:20:10,876
-Cool.
-[vendor] Yeah, and it's folded.

441
00:20:11,419 --> 00:20:14,463
Colors and the pattern came
from different areas.

442
00:20:15,047 --> 00:20:19,051
So this is from…
This was originated in Ganado, Arizona.

443
00:20:19,135 --> 00:20:22,638
-Arizona, yeah.
-That, that brown and stuff is Burnham,

444
00:20:22,722 --> 00:20:24,974
-which is Burnham, New Mexico…
-Uh-huh.

445
00:20:25,057 --> 00:20:28,853
So, um, there were
trading posts at those places.

446
00:20:29,478 --> 00:20:31,814
And they would bring their rugs in

447
00:20:31,897 --> 00:20:36,527
and the trading post owners would order
things that looked like this

448
00:20:36,610 --> 00:20:38,696
for tourists and people to buy.

449
00:20:39,697 --> 00:20:42,032
It's called Burnham Pictorial.

450
00:20:42,116 --> 00:20:44,744
-Burnham is the pattern.
-Mm-hmm.

451
00:20:44,827 --> 00:20:48,038
-And then Pictorial means the people.
-[Genta] Oh, nice.

452
00:20:48,122 --> 00:20:49,874
-[vendor] So natural dyes.
-[Genta, Machu] Natural.

453
00:20:49,957 --> 00:20:54,003
-[vendor] And there's some that might…
-Ah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

454
00:20:54,086 --> 00:20:55,671
[in Japanese] I would've never known.

455
00:20:55,755 --> 00:20:57,673
I thought these patterns were
random, you know?

456
00:20:57,757 --> 00:20:59,967
-It's depicting people.
-My first time seeing ones like these.

457
00:21:00,551 --> 00:21:03,637
[in English]
And all rugs are woven with a flaw.

458
